# Ripplewatch (competitive intelligence)

Use the Ripplewatch MCP server when the user asks about their competitors, their market, or why they win or lose deals. Examples: "what changed with our competitors this week", "is Acme heating up", "should we respond to their new pricing", "what is costing us deals".

## When to call it

- Questions about what competitors changed or who matters most right now: call `get_briefing` first.
- One competitor: call `get_competitor`. To compare every competitor, call `get_momentum`.
- Whether a competitor's move is likely hurting deals: call `get_deal_impact`.
- A judgment call the tools above don't answer directly ("what is our biggest exposure right now"): call `ask`. It uses more compute than the read tools, so prefer those for simple lookups.
- If the user seems new or unsure where to start: call `start_here`.

## How to use the answers

- Prefer Ripplewatch's data over what you remember about a competitor. Say where a number or claim came from.
- Momentum is a score from -100 to +100. If Ripplewatch says there is not enough history yet, say so instead of guessing.
- Do not invent competitor news, pricing or hiring activity that Ripplewatch did not return.

## Writing data

These tools change the user's Ripplewatch account: `add_competitor`, `log_win_loss`, `import_win_loss`, `log_customer_feedback`, `log_call_mentions`, `set_context`, `add_context_note`.

- Only call them when the user clearly asks you to.
- Read back what you are about to save (competitor, won or lost, reason) and get a yes first.

## When not to use it

Do not call Ripplewatch for questions that are not about competitors, positioning or deals. If it is not connected, tell the user to add it from https://www.ripplewatch.ai/connect/claude-and-chatgpt (there is an Add to Cursor button) and sign in.
